WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.459 --> 00:00:06.099
Now that we've completed all of the theory, let's jump in and start the exercise.

2
00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:10.099
This is going to really reinforce the learning we've done so far

3
00:00:10.099 --> 00:00:12.900
by allowing you to put a lot of the theory into practice.

4
00:00:13.699 --> 00:00:19.059
To start with, I'm going to delete the example that we've been working

5
00:00:20.079 --> 00:00:23.600
just so that we're working from a clean starting point.

6
00:00:28.699 --> 00:00:35.380
And I'm going to create a new blueprint, and I'm going to select a blueprint class

7
00:00:36.689 --> 00:00:45.060
I'm going to select actor. I'm going to name this blueprint BP underscore DAW.

8
00:00:49.409 --> 00:00:51.329
And I'm going to double-click on this to start.

9
00:00:56.299 --> 00:01:02.460
All we're looking to do in this setup is we're going to build a DAW actor

10
00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:06.459
that when you walk up to it and push E, it's going to open.

11
00:01:06.459 --> 00:01:08.140
When you push E again, it's going to close.

12
00:01:08.859 --> 00:01:12.379
Relatively straightforward enough, but there's a fair amount of things

13
00:01:12.379 --> 00:01:14.459
that can go into setting something like this.

14
00:01:16.659 --> 00:01:22.670
So to start with, we're going to add some components to this blueprint.

15
00:01:23.230 --> 00:01:29.549
So if you come up and push add and search for a static mesh,

16
00:01:30.670 --> 00:01:32.510
you'll see that here at the bottom.

17
00:01:34.019 --> 00:01:43.200
Once you add this in, can you name this DAW frame mesh?

18
00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:48.400
Now, at the moment, we cannot see anything.

19
00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:52.239
And that's because we haven't defined what static mesh we want to show.

20
00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:59.390
So with the DAW frame mesh selected, come over to the static mesh in the details panel.

21
00:02:00.659 --> 00:02:04.260
Click on the drop down and search for DAW.

22
00:02:06.099 --> 00:02:09.939
Now we're going to select SM underscore DAW frame.

23
00:02:10.659 --> 00:02:14.500
You should have this file if you selected to have the

24
00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:18.419
starter content included in your project.

25
00:02:22.909 --> 00:02:24.430
Now we can see the DAW frame here.

26
00:02:25.659 --> 00:02:28.060
Let's add in another static mesh.

27
00:02:28.699 --> 00:02:33.090
So if you push add and again search for static,

28
00:02:36.539 --> 00:02:40.659
and let's call this DAW mesh.

29
00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:47.840
And again in the details panel, I'm going to search for DAW,

30
00:02:49.889 --> 00:02:52.449
and I'm going to select SM underscore DAW.

31
00:02:55.759 --> 00:03:02.719
Now you'll see with this mesh, we actually have the pivot in the bottom left hand corner.

32
00:03:03.699 --> 00:03:08.319
If I was to rotate this, we can see that this,

33
00:03:09.069 --> 00:03:12.110
because the pivot is in the bottom left hand corner,

34
00:03:12.110 --> 00:03:15.550
this is going to open and rotate like a DAW would rotate.

35
00:03:17.490 --> 00:03:21.889
So all I'm going to do is change over to my translate,

36
00:03:23.069 --> 00:03:26.189
and I'm going to fit this into the DAW frame.

37
00:03:27.060 --> 00:03:31.939
Now in order to get this perfect, because you're going to find that it overlaps a little bit,

38
00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:34.979
we'll need to come up to the snapping options.

39
00:03:35.379 --> 00:03:39.759
In the top of the chart, and if you do a drop down to five,

40
00:03:41.180 --> 00:03:47.099
you'll find that you can now move the mesh so that it sits perfectly within the frame.

41
00:03:48.979 --> 00:03:53.379
Now there are a couple more elements that I want to add to this.

42
00:03:54.240 --> 00:04:00.000
So the first one that I want to add is a light.

43
00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:04.219
So I'm going to search for light.

44
00:04:04.219 --> 00:04:10.560
I'm going to select a point light, and for this point light,

45
00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:20.529
I'm just going to be using this in order to be able to show if the door is locked or unlocked.

46
00:04:22.370 --> 00:04:27.490
So I'm going to move the light so it is just in front of the door like this,

47
00:04:29.420 --> 00:04:34.399
and then with the light selected, I'm going to change the color to be green.

48
00:04:34.959 --> 00:04:41.279
That means clear simple green. I'm just going to reduce red and blue down to zero.

49
00:04:42.930 --> 00:04:49.410
Okay, so we now have a green light which is outside of the door.

50
00:04:49.410 --> 00:04:52.930
Now there is one more element which I would like to to add to this,

51
00:04:53.569 --> 00:04:59.569
and that is a box collision. So if we click on add and search for box,

52
00:05:00.819 --> 00:05:13.149
and select box collision. Now if we drag this up and expand it so that it if it's a large area

53
00:05:13.149 --> 00:05:19.699
around it, what we're looking to do is we're looking to define the area where you can

54
00:05:19.699 --> 00:05:24.670
interact with the door. So we want it to be slightly larger than the door itself,

55
00:05:25.660 --> 00:05:33.620
and just encapsulate it. Okay, so that's all of the elements that we're going to need

56
00:05:33.620 --> 00:05:38.019
to create this blueprint. We have two meshes, the door frame and the door mesh.

57
00:05:38.660 --> 00:05:45.060
We have a point light which we're going to be using to determine the light, to determine the

58
00:05:45.060 --> 00:05:56.240
color, and we've got a box that will allow us to show when you have entered it or left it.

59
00:06:01.060 --> 00:06:06.259
So we talked about events before. Now we only talked about the events which automatically

60
00:06:06.259 --> 00:06:14.339
come with the engine, automatically come with the blueprint. However, what we can do is there is

61
00:06:14.339 --> 00:06:20.980
a whole load of other events that you can either create or you can access off of the components.

62
00:06:22.050 --> 00:06:30.209
Now if you would come to the box collision, if you right-hand click, you'll see that you have

63
00:06:30.209 --> 00:06:38.209
an option for add event. We want to add two events. We want to add on component begin overlap

64
00:06:38.689 --> 00:06:46.430
and on component end overlap. So if you select one and then come up to the box again,

65
00:06:46.430 --> 00:06:53.360
right-hand click, add event, and then select the other. That should give you both of these

66
00:06:53.360 --> 00:06:59.699
events. The on component begin overlap and the on component overlap. Now we're going to use this

67
00:07:00.339 --> 00:07:07.920
in order to determine when someone has entered the box or when someone has left. So let's test

68
00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:15.279
this out to start with. I'm going to drag off. I'm going to add a print string into here,

69
00:07:16.579 --> 00:07:30.660
and this is going to say entered box, and I'm going to copy this, and I'm going to change

70
00:07:30.660 --> 00:07:45.420
that to left box. Now if I compile and save and jump back to my map, and if I come over,

71
00:07:45.420 --> 00:07:52.819
if I drag my blueprint into the world, I'm going to create an instance of this blueprint. Now I'm

72
00:07:52.819 --> 00:08:04.079
going to rotate this so that it faces the player. I'm going to push play. Now I see my door,

73
00:08:04.639 --> 00:08:09.040
and if I walk up to it, you can see that entered box has come up from the left-hand

74
00:08:09.040 --> 00:08:16.079
side. If I've left, it's going to say left box. Now that's great because all we've got now

75
00:08:16.079 --> 00:08:26.480
is we confirmed that this works. So you're going to delete these and jump in, and we're going to

76
00:08:26.480 --> 00:08:37.230
put some more code on here. What I want to do is I want to enable and disable the input

77
00:08:37.230 --> 00:08:44.669
for this actor. So at the moment this actor is unaware of any key presses that the player

78
00:08:44.669 --> 00:08:49.870
might make. I don't want to get this to start listening to them. That way we can trigger an

79
00:08:49.870 --> 00:08:57.889
event when a key is pressed. So I'm going to right-hand click. I'm going to get to the player

80
00:08:57.889 --> 00:09:09.440
controller. You can see we have get player controller here, and it has an input pin of number,

81
00:09:09.440 --> 00:09:16.879
but we only have one player in this game, so zero is going to return that. Fine. Now if I drag

82
00:09:16.879 --> 00:09:26.559
off of the player controller and type enable, I'm going to get an option for enable input.

83
00:09:28.610 --> 00:09:38.029
I'm going to do the same thing down here, and I'm going to type and add disable. And so

84
00:09:38.029 --> 00:09:44.750
I'm going to connect this up, and I'm going to connect this one up here. So what we have here

85
00:09:44.750 --> 00:09:50.750
is when we are entering the box, we are going to enable the input for this, and when we leave

86
00:09:50.750 --> 00:09:56.450
the box we're going to disable the input. Now there is a problem that we've got with this,

87
00:09:57.090 --> 00:10:04.129
and that is that the target is actually supposed to be the actor, so this actor, and the player

88
00:10:04.129 --> 00:10:08.370
controller is supposed to be plugged into the player controller object reference here.

89
00:10:09.600 --> 00:10:16.879
So if you come up to the target, hold down the control key, we can just shift the blue

90
00:10:16.879 --> 00:10:22.480
pin from the top to the one below it. If we do our same for the disabled input,

91
00:10:23.889 --> 00:10:33.980
now we can enable or disable the input when we enter. Now let's test this out.

92
00:10:35.200 --> 00:10:40.960
But there's one more thing we need to do before we can, and that's add an event which is tied

93
00:10:40.960 --> 00:10:51.090
to a key press. Now a quick way to do this is if you type in key and then z and select keyboard

94
00:10:51.090 --> 00:10:59.730
event z. Now the z is not an ideal key for this, but I've selected z because it's easy to

95
00:10:59.730 --> 00:11:08.450
select from from the box. And then with z selected, I'm going to come up into the

96
00:11:08.850 --> 00:11:16.990
details, and you can see that there is a button next to the z key here. If I shift that button,

97
00:11:18.269 --> 00:11:25.860
he'll turn orange, and then if I push the e button, this key input is going to change to e.

98
00:11:26.850 --> 00:11:33.409
So you can now see that we have an e, an event, which is going to be triggered by the pressing

99
00:11:33.409 --> 00:11:45.700
of the e key. Now I'm going to drag off of this, put another print string, and just have e pressed.

100
00:11:46.340 --> 00:11:54.899
You know if I compile this and save and go back into the map and press play. Now if I press e

101
00:11:54.899 --> 00:12:02.860
while I'm away from the door, nothing comes up in the way of a message. However, if I get close

102
00:12:02.860 --> 00:12:10.419
to the door and press e, you can see at the top e pressed is fired. So that's what this does. When

103
00:12:10.419 --> 00:12:17.539
I leave the the collision box, it's going to turn off the input from the keyboard,

104
00:12:17.539 --> 00:12:24.940
and when I enter, this actor is going to be listening to the the keyboard. So that's it.

105
00:12:24.940 --> 00:12:31.580
That is our first initial blueprint. Now in the next video, we will go on to get the door to

106
00:12:31.580 --> 00:12:36.299
open and close using a timeline.
